    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>New package: bun 1.4.0~git20260808.52bf09cb

Bun is an all-in-one JavaScript runtime and toolkit with a bundler, test
runner and Node.js-compatible package manager. Requested as a dependency of
opencode and OpenTUI, which cannot be packaged without it.

This is a snapshot of the Rust rewrite rather than the 1.3.14 release.
1.3.14 is the last release written in Zig, that branch is no longer
developed, and packaging it would mean shipping a prebuilt Zig compiler.
The commit is the one upstream's CI published as a canary build, not the
tip of main. It will be switched to a release tarball once 1.4.0 is tagged.

Everything is built from source with what Factory ships:

- The Rust that Factory ships (&gt;= 1.97), not the nightly upstream pins.
  Every unstable feature bun uses is present in it and RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1
  opens the gate, as rustc's own bootstrap does.
- JavaScriptCore is compiled from the WebKit source in Source1 rather than
  taken as upstream's prebuilt engine.
- The build is fully offline: the npm dependencies and the WebKit and LLVM
  inputs the build would otherwise fetch are prepared by two generators
  (bun_prefetch, bun_webkit) that the maintainer runs on a version bump,
  documented in README.SUSE-maint.

The one thing not built from source is the bootstrap bun. 24 code-generation
steps in the build need an existing bun to run, so a bun binary is needed to
compile bun, exactly as for rust, ghc, sbcl and deno. It is Source100/101,
marked NoSource, used only at build time, and nothing from it ends up in the
binary. A %bcond bootstrap is already in the spec to retire it as soon as
Factory has a bun to build the next one with; the spec carries the mandatory
&quot;#KEEP NOSOURCE DEBUGINFO&quot; comment.

ExclusiveArch: x86_64 aarch64 — upstream supports no other architecture.
Both are green in devel:tools:building.

rpmlint: 0 errors, 1 warning —
  W: position-independent-executable-suggested /usr/bin/bun
This is deliberate upstream: bun builds itself and JavaScriptCore with
-fno-pic -fno-pie -no-pie, because the JIT depends on it. It cannot be
turned on without patching out the JIT.

The license is a compound expression covering the whole statically linked
executable, not just bun's MIT code: it embeds JavaScriptCore and tinycc
(LGPL-2.1), the Servo CSS crates (MPL-2.0), libjpeg-turbo (IJG), the SQLite
amalgamation (blessing) and unicode-ident (Unicode-3.0), among others.
LGPL-2.1 section 6 is satisfied because the WebKit source is Source1 and
ships in the src.rpm. Every statically linked C/C++ library is declared with
Provides: bundled(); the vendored Rust crates are left to the automatic
generator. None of the C/C++ ones can be unbundled: each is pinned to an
exact commit, several are upstream forks, and the JavaScript engine is a
fork of WebKit.

Six patches, all local build fixes, described one by one in the changelog
and in README.SUSE-maint. Three of them are what it took to get rpm to
extract a debuginfo package from the ~400 MB executable; the resulting
bun-debuginfo and bun-debugsource are complete.

_constraints are included: the build reaches 10 GB of build root before the
final link.</comment>
